{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01MYupFhtDYvx4NZ76iKjHX8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! cdylib surface of the translation.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! The C source, when compiled as a shared library, exports exactly two\n4\t//! symbols (`print_hex` is `static`, so it stays internal):\n5\t//!\n6\t//! ```text\n7\t//! T driver\n8\t//! T main\n9\t//! ```\n10\t//!\n11\t//! This crate re-exports the same two symbols with C ABI linkage and the same\n12\t//! signatures so that a caller loading either `.so` with `dlopen`/`dlsym`\n13\t//! observes identical behaviour.\n14\t\n15\tuse std::os::raw::c_int;\n16\t\n17\tpub mod imp;\n18\t\n19\t/// `void driver(int floors)`\n20\t#[no_mangle]\n21\tpub extern \"C\" fn driver(floors: c_int) {\n22\t    imp::driver(floors as i32);\n23\t}\n24\t\n25\t/// `int main()`\n26\t///\n27\t/// The C shared library exports its `main` just like any other non-static\n28\t/// function, so the translation exports it too. Calling it reads one `%d`\n29\t/// conversion from stdin and prints the struct bytes, exactly as the C\n30\t/// `main` does.\n31\t#[no_mangle]\n32\tpub extern \"C\" fn main() -> c_int {\n33\t    imp::run_main() as c_int\n34\t}\n35\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01P2pkzbSTBNJHRYmLakmKP1","session_id":"f840c14a-a681-4b88-88b0-66464adaa41c","uuid":"3417254b-f2af-402c-9cbf-eb5c0c35f64d","timestamp":"2026-08-18T10:36:27.064Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit C vs Rust semantics"}
