{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"ab647afda1b5ba9d5","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01P2pkzbSTBNJHRYmLakmKP1","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-h17CiR/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-h17CiR-translated-rust/f840c14a-a681-4b88-88b0-66464adaa41c/tasks/ab647afda1b5ba9d5.output","summary":"## Findings\n\nAll four divergences below share one root cause: the translation performs I/O through Rust's `std::io` (`std::io::stdin()` / `std::io::stdout()`) instead of libc's `stdin`/`stdout` FILE objects that `scanf`/`printf` use. The *pure* `scanf(\"%d\")` value semantics are, as far as I can measure, exactly right — the remaining bugs are all in the stream layer.\n\nProbes/binaries live in `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-h17CiR/tmp/audit/` (gcc 11.5 `-O2`, rustc 1.94 `-O`); nothing under the working dir was modified.\n\n---\n\n### D1 — CONFIRMED. `main()` prints a different line: Rust's stdin is not libc's `stdin`\n\nThe C `.so`'s `main` reads through the process's libc `stdin`, so it shares buffer and position with a host that also uses C stdio. The Rust cdylib reads through a private `std::io::Stdin` (`src/imp.rs:270-271`), which is a completely separate object.\n\nHost (`h4.c`) does `scanf(\"%d\",&y)` and *then* calls the library's exported `main()`. **stdin = `5 7`**:\n\n```\nmode=1 libc_driver.so       host-before: scanf=1 y=5|07000000030000000000000000000040|\nmode=1 librust_driver.so    host-before: scanf=1 y=5|00000000030000000000000000000040|\n```\n\nC: host consumes `5`, `main()` consumes `7` → `07…`. Rust: the host's `scanf` pulled the whole fd into libc's buffer; Rust's `main()` sees an empty fd, `scanf_d` returns EOF, `x` stays `0` → `00…`. **The exported symbol's own printed output differs.**\n\nReverse order (`main()` first, then host `scanf`), same input:\n\n```\nmode=0 libc_driver.so       host-after: scanf=1 y=7|05000000030000000000000000000040|\nmode=0 librust_driver.so    05000000030000000000000000000040|host-after: scanf=-1 y=-999|\n```\n\nC hands the host `y=7`; Rust hands it EOF and leaves `y` untouched.\n\n---\n\n### D2 — CONFIRMED. glibc restores stdin's file offset at exit; the Rust binary does not\n\nThis one hits the **standalone binary**, not just the cdylib. glibc's `exit` → `_IO_cleanup` → `_IO_unbuffer_all` → `_IO_new_file_sync` issues `lseek(fd, _IO_read_ptr - _IO_read_end, SEEK_CUR)`, rewinding the descriptor to the *logical* stream position. Rust's `BufReader` (8192-byte buffer) never seeks back.\n\nFile `seek.txt` = `42 hello world\\n`, run as `{ ./driver; cat; } < seek.txt`:\n\n```\ndriver_c   stdout=2a000000030000000000000000000040\n  leftover=[ hello world]\ndriver_rs  stdout=2a000000030000000000000000000040\n  leftover=[]\n```\n\nParent-measured `lseek(fd,0,SEEK_CUR)` after the child exits (probe `parent2.c`):\n\n```\ninput                            C off  RS off\nb'42 hello'                      2      8       <== DIVERGES\nb'42x rest'                      2      8       <== DIVERGES\nb'   42 rest'                    5      10      <== DIVERGES\nb'42\\nrest'                       2      7       <== DIVERGES\nb'-42, rest'                     3      9       <== DIVERGES\nb'- 42 rest'                     1      9       <== DIVERGES\nb'abc rest'                      0      8       <== DIVERGES\nb'0x10 rest'                     1      9       <== DIVERGES\nb'\\n\\n\\n5 rest'                   4      9       <== DIVERGES\nb'999999999999999999999999 rest' 24     29      <== DIVERGES\nb'42'                            2      2       (same)\nb''                              0      0       (same)\n```\n\nC's offset is exactly \"characters consumed by the conversion\", with the `ungetc`'d terminator *not* counted (note `- 42 rest` → 1, `abc rest` → 0). Verified the mechanism directly: mid-run the C child's own `lseek(0)` reads 15 (full readahead), and only after exit does the parent see 2.\n\nSame divergence through the exported `main()` in a dlopen host that exits normally: `42 hello world` → C off=2, RS off=14.\n\nRelated, for **non-seekable** stdin where no restore is possible, the readahead size still differs — glibc uses `st_blksize` (4096 here), Rust uses 8192. Piping 20002 bytes: C leaves 15906 unread on the pipe, Rust leaves 11810.\n\n---\n\n### D3 — CONFIRMED. Output ordering / durability: Rust's stdout is not libc's `stdout`\n\n`src/imp.rs:84-94` writes and *flushes* immediately into Rust's stdout. The C code leaves 33 bytes in libc's fully-buffered `stdout` until process exit, in the same buffer the host uses.\n\nHost prints `HOST-BEFORE`, calls `main()`, prints `HOST-AFTER` (`h2.c`, stdout = pipe):\n\n```\nmode=0 libc_driver.so         -> HOST-BEFORE|07000000030000000000000000000040|HOST-AFTER|\nmode=0 librust_driver.so      -> 07000000030000000000000000000040|HOST-BEFORE|HOST-AFTER|\n```\n\nHost calls `_exit(0)` after `main()` (skipping stdio cleanup):\n\n```\nmode=1 libc_driver.so         -> (nothing at all)\nmode=1 librust_driver.so      -> 07000000030000000000000000000040|\n```\n\nC loses the line entirely; Rust emits it. Same asymmetry for `abort()`/`_exit` anywhere after the call.\n\nWrite granularity also differs whenever stdout is unbuffered. Host does `setvbuf(stdout,NULL,_IONBF,0)`, then `driver(1)` with stdout a pipe; the reader logs each `read()`:\n\n```\n=== libc_driver.so ===     read#1=4 read#2=29\n=== librust_driver.so ===  read#1=33\n```\n\n(C makes 17 separate `printf` calls; Rust makes one 33-byte write.)\n\n---\n\n### D4 — CONFIRMED (concurrency). `print_hex` is atomic in Rust, interleavable in C\n\nEach of C's 17 `printf` calls takes and releases the stdout lock, so two threads calling the exported `driver` interleave at 2-hex-digit granularity. Rust builds the whole line and writes it under a single `StdoutLock`, so it can never interleave. Two threads × 4000 calls of `driver(0x11111111)` / `driver(0x22222222)`:\n\n```\nlibc_driver.so       3298     <- distinct output lines\nlibrust_driver.so    2\n```\n\nSample C lines: `0000000000001100000000114011`, `00000000000000220022222203004000`. The Rust version emits only the two clean lines, always. (Non-deterministic, but the reachable output *set* differs.)\n\n---\n\n## (b) Checked and found EQUIVALENT\n\n- **`scanf(\"%d\")` value semantics — no divergence found in ~6100 differential cases.** 84 hand-picked + 4000 random-byte inputs (single run) and 840 numeric-boundary inputs, all byte-identical stdout + exit status; 1200 random + 150 targeted inputs through repeated `main()` calls (n = 1…8) in the dlopen host, identical stdout + stderr + status. Specifically verified equivalent:\n  - whitespace set (` \\t\\n\\v\\f\\r`), including leading-whitespace-only and EOF-during-skip;\n  - sign handling: `+`/`-`, `--3`, `+-5`, `- 5`, `++5`, sign-then-EOF, sign reused from pushback (`5-` → 5 then matching failure);\n  - **base-10 only**: `0x10` → `x = 0` with `x10` left unread (glibc's base-detection block is inert for base 10); `010` → 10, not 8;\n  - digit accumulation with long leading-zero runs (`0`×50 + `7`);\n  - **`strtol` saturation then `(int)` truncation**: swept ±3 around 2³¹, 2³², 2⁶³, 2⁶⁴, 10¹⁹, 10²⁰ plus 600 random 1–45-digit strings. e.g. `9223372036854775808` → LONG_MAX → `(int)` = −1 → `ffffffff…`; `-9223372036854775809` → LONG_MIN → `(int)` = 0. Rust's `checked_mul/add/sub` + saturate-to-`i64::MIN/MAX` + `as i32` reproduces this exactly, including the \"once overflowed, stay overflowed\" behavior;\n  - matching failure leaves `x` at its initial 0 (return value is discarded by `main`, so 1/0/EOF is unobservable);\n  - terminator pushback and sticky EOF (the two already-fixed items) behave identically across up to 8 chained `main()` calls.\n- **Struct object representation.** 3022 `driver()` arguments (all i32 boundaries + 3000 random) produce byte-identical hex. Layout confirmed: `floors`@0, `bedrooms`@4, `bathrooms`@8, size 16, **no padding**, so `= {0}` vs `House::zeroed()` is moot. `2.0` → `0000000000000040` LE.\n- **`%02x` on `unsigned char`** (default-promoted to `int`, printed unsigned) == Rust `{:02x}` on `u8` for all 256 values.\n- **`print_hex`'s `int len`.** Only ever called with `sizeof(house)` = 16; no `size_t`→`int` truncation, no sign issue, loop bound identical.\n- **Exit statuses / error paths, all identical (rc 0):** closed stdin, directory as stdin (`EISDIR`), closed stdout (`EBADF`), `> /dev/full` (`ENOSPC`). The read-error path correctly does *not* set `EOF_SEEN` — glibc's `_IO_file_underflow` gates on `_IO_EOF_SEEN` only, not the error indicator, so it retries too.\n- **SIGPIPE.** With a pipe that has no reader at all, both are killed by signal 13 (`signaled=1 sig=13`) — the `SIG_DFL` restore in `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-h17CiR/translated_rust/src/main.rs:34` works, and the differing flush *timing* does not change the outcome.\n- **ABI of the exported `main`.** Calling it as `main(argc, argv, envp)` (legal against C's `int main()` identifier-list prototype) returns 0 and prints the same line in both.\n- **Locale.** Neither program calls `setlocale`, so both use the C locale's `isspace`/`isdigit`; no `'`/`I` flag is in play, so no grouping or i18n-digit path.\n\n## Suggested direction\n\nD1–D3 are not fixable by tweaking `scanf_d`. Faithfulness requires the translation to go through libc's stdio: `extern \"C\" { fn scanf(...); fn printf(...); }` (or at minimum libc `getc`/`ungetc` on `stdin` and `fwrite` to `stdout` with no explicit flush). That single change removes D1, D2, and D3's ordering/durability aspects at once; D3's write granularity and D4 additionally require reproducing the 17 separate `printf` calls rather than one buffered `write_all`.","usage":{"total_tokens":72904,"tool_uses":31,"duration_ms":729026},"uuid":"b57072b6-b1b2-447e-a6b8-a8ba7abb31a7","session_id":"f840c14a-a681-4b88-88b0-66464adaa41c"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01P2pkzbSTBNJHRYmLakmKP1","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Findings\n\nAll four divergences below share one root cause: the translation performs I/O through Rust's `std::io` (`std::io::stdin()` / `std::io::stdout()`) instead of libc's `stdin`/`stdout` FILE objects that `scanf`/`printf` use. The *pure* `scanf(\"%d\")` value semantics are, as far as I can measure, exactly right — the remaining bugs are all in the stream layer.\n\nProbes/binaries live in `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-h17CiR/tmp/audit/` (gcc 11.5 `-O2`, rustc 1.94 `-O`); nothing under the working dir was modified.\n\n---\n\n### D1 — CONFIRMED. `main()` prints a different line: Rust's stdin is not libc's `stdin`\n\nThe C `.so`'s `main` reads through the process's libc `stdin`, so it shares buffer and position with a host that also uses C stdio. The Rust cdylib reads through a private `std::io::Stdin` (`src/imp.rs:270-271`), which is a completely separate object.\n\nHost (`h4.c`) does `scanf(\"%d\",&y)` and *then* calls the library's exported `main()`. **stdin = `5 7`**:\n\n```\nmode=1 libc_driver.so       host-before: scanf=1 y=5|07000000030000000000000000000040|\nmode=1 librust_driver.so    host-before: scanf=1 y=5|00000000030000000000000000000040|\n```\n\nC: host consumes `5`, `main()` consumes `7` → `07…`. Rust: the host's `scanf` pulled the whole fd into libc's buffer; Rust's `main()` sees an empty fd, `scanf_d` returns EOF, `x` stays `0` → `00…`. **The exported symbol's own printed output differs.**\n\nReverse order (`main()` first, then host `scanf`), same input:\n\n```\nmode=0 libc_driver.so       host-after: scanf=1 y=7|05000000030000000000000000000040|\nmode=0 librust_driver.so    05000000030000000000000000000040|host-after: scanf=-1 y=-999|\n```\n\nC hands the host `y=7`; Rust hands it EOF and leaves `y` untouched.\n\n---\n\n### D2 — CONFIRMED. glibc restores stdin's file offset at exit; the Rust binary does not\n\nThis one hits the **standalone binary**, not just the cdylib. glibc's `exit` → `_IO_cleanup` → `_IO_unbuffer_all` → `_IO_new_file_sync` issues `lseek(fd, _IO_read_ptr - _IO_read_end, SEEK_CUR)`, rewinding the descriptor to the *logical* stream position. Rust's `BufReader` (8192-byte buffer) never seeks back.\n\nFile `seek.txt` = `42 hello world\\n`, run as `{ ./driver; cat; } < seek.txt`:\n\n```\ndriver_c   stdout=2a000000030000000000000000000040\n  leftover=[ hello world]\ndriver_rs  stdout=2a000000030000000000000000000040\n  leftover=[]\n```\n\nParent-measured `lseek(fd,0,SEEK_CUR)` after the child exits (probe `parent2.c`):\n\n```\ninput                            C off  RS off\nb'42 hello'                      2      8       <== DIVERGES\nb'42x rest'                      2      8       <== DIVERGES\nb'   42 rest'                    5      10      <== DIVERGES\nb'42\\nrest'                       2      7       <== DIVERGES\nb'-42, rest'                     3      9       <== DIVERGES\nb'- 42 rest'                     1      9       <== DIVERGES\nb'abc rest'                      0      8       <== DIVERGES\nb'0x10 rest'                     1      9       <== DIVERGES\nb'\\n\\n\\n5 rest'                   4      9       <== DIVERGES\nb'999999999999999999999999 rest' 24     29      <== DIVERGES\nb'42'                            2      2       (same)\nb''                              0      0       (same)\n```\n\nC's offset is exactly \"characters consumed by the conversion\", with the `ungetc`'d terminator *not* counted (note `- 42 rest` → 1, `abc rest` → 0). Verified the mechanism directly: mid-run the C child's own `lseek(0)` reads 15 (full readahead), and only after exit does the parent see 2.\n\nSame divergence through the exported `main()` in a dlopen host that exits normally: `42 hello world` → C off=2, RS off=14.\n\nRelated, for **non-seekable** stdin where no restore is possible, the readahead size still differs — glibc uses `st_blksize` (4096 here), Rust uses 8192. Piping 20002 bytes: C leaves 15906 unread on the pipe, Rust leaves 11810.\n\n---\n\n### D3 — CONFIRMED. Output ordering / durability: Rust's stdout is not libc's `stdout`\n\n`src/imp.rs:84-94` writes and *flushes* immediately into Rust's stdout. The C code leaves 33 bytes in libc's fully-buffered `stdout` until process exit, in the same buffer the host uses.\n\nHost prints `HOST-BEFORE`, calls `main()`, prints `HOST-AFTER` (`h2.c`, stdout = pipe):\n\n```\nmode=0 libc_driver.so         -> HOST-BEFORE|07000000030000000000000000000040|HOST-AFTER|\nmode=0 librust_driver.so      -> 07000000030000000000000000000040|HOST-BEFORE|HOST-AFTER|\n```\n\nHost calls `_exit(0)` after `main()` (skipping stdio cleanup):\n\n```\nmode=1 libc_driver.so         -> (nothing at all)\nmode=1 librust_driver.so      -> 07000000030000000000000000000040|\n```\n\nC loses the line entirely; Rust emits it. Same asymmetry for `abort()`/`_exit` anywhere after the call.\n\nWrite granularity also differs whenever stdout is unbuffered. Host does `setvbuf(stdout,NULL,_IONBF,0)`, then `driver(1)` with stdout a pipe; the reader logs each `read()`:\n\n```\n=== libc_driver.so ===     read#1=4 read#2=29\n=== librust_driver.so ===  read#1=33\n```\n\n(C makes 17 separate `printf` calls; Rust makes one 33-byte write.)\n\n---\n\n### D4 — CONFIRMED (concurrency). `print_hex` is atomic in Rust, interleavable in C\n\nEach of C's 17 `printf` calls takes and releases the stdout lock, so two threads calling the exported `driver` interleave at 2-hex-digit granularity. Rust builds the whole line and writes it under a single `StdoutLock`, so it can never interleave. Two threads × 4000 calls of `driver(0x11111111)` / `driver(0x22222222)`:\n\n```\nlibc_driver.so       3298     <- distinct output lines\nlibrust_driver.so    2\n```\n\nSample C lines: `0000000000001100000000114011`, `00000000000000220022222203004000`. The Rust version emits only the two clean lines, always. (