{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01WotYDbC7acfuk8QmMDvhUF","type":"tool_result","content":"test result: ok. 0 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.00s\ntest result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.31s\ntest result: ok. 9 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.12s\ntest result: ok. 16 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 3.07s\ntest result: ok. 5 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.75s\ntest result: ok. 18 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 1.04s\ntest result: ok. 5 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.13s\ntest result: ok. 5 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.17s\ntest result: ok. 5 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.08s\n=== final source review ===\n 154 src/imp.rs\n  39 src/lib.rs\n  39 src/main.rs\n  51 c_src/src/main.c\n 283 total\n\n//! Executable entry point, mirroring the C program built by\n//! `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` (`add_executable(driver src/main.c)`).\n//!\n//! The implementation lives in `imp.rs`, which is also compiled into the\n//! cdylib. It is included by path rather than through the library crate\n//! because the library exports a C-ABI symbol named `main`, which would\n//! collide with this binary's entry point at link time.\n\nuse std::os::raw::c_int;\n\n#[path = \"imp.rs\"]\nmod imp;\n\n/// `SIGPIPE` on Linux.\nconst SIGPIPE: c_int = 13;\n/// `SIG_DFL`; glibc's `sighandler_t` is pointer-sized.\nconst SIG_DFL: usize = 0;\n\nextern \"C\" {\n    fn signal(signum: c_int, handler: usize) -> usize;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    // A C program starts with the default disposition for SIGPIPE, so writing\n    // to a pipe whose reader has gone away kills it with signal 13. The Rust\n    // runtime instead installs SIG_IGN before `main`, which would turn that\n    // into an ignored `EPIPE` and an exit status of 0. Restore the C startup\n    // state so the translated program dies exactly like the original.\n    //\n    // This is deliberately *not* done in the cdylib: a shared library must not\n    // change the host process's signal disposition, and the C shared library\n    // does not do so either.\n    unsafe {\n        signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL);\n    }\n\n    // The C `main` returns 0; the process exit status is therefore always 0.\n    let _ = imp::run_main();\n}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f840c14a-a681-4b88-88b0-66464adaa41c","uuid":"4844a54e-aa8b-44d1-8b21-afc9b72d4185","timestamp":"2026-08-18T10:55:49.738Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"test result: ok. 0 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.00s\ntest result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.31s\ntest result: ok. 9 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.12s\ntest result: ok. 16 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 3.07s\ntest result: ok. 5 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.75s\ntest result: ok. 18 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 1.04s\ntest result: ok. 5 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.13s\ntest result: ok. 5 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.17s\ntest result: ok. 5 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.08s\n=== final source review ===\n 154 src/imp.rs\n  39 src/lib.rs\n  39 src/main.rs\n  51 c_src/src/main.c\n 283 total\n\n//! Executable entry point, mirroring the C program built by\n//! `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` (`add_executable(driver src/main.c)`).\n//!\n//! The implementation lives in `imp.rs`, which is also compiled into the\n//! cdylib. It is included by path rather than through the library crate\n//! because the library exports a C-ABI symbol named `main`, which would\n//! collide with this binary's entry point at link time.\n\nuse std::os::raw::c_int;\n\n#[path = \"imp.rs\"]\nmod imp;\n\n/// `SIGPIPE` on Linux.\nconst SIGPIPE: c_int = 13;\n/// `SIG_DFL`; glibc's `sighandler_t` is pointer-sized.\nconst SIG_DFL: usize = 0;\n\nextern \"C\" {\n    fn signal(signum: c_int, handler: usize) -> usize;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    // A C program starts with the default disposition for SIGPIPE, so writing\n    // to a pipe whose reader has gone away kills it with signal 13. The Rust\n    // runtime instead installs SIG_IGN before `main`, which would turn that\n    // into an ignored `EPIPE` and an exit status of 0. Restore the C startup\n    // state so the translated program dies exactly like the original.\n    //\n    // This is deliberately *not* done in the cdylib: a shared library must not\n    // change the host process's signal disposition, and the C shared library\n    // does not do so either.\n    unsafe {\n        signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL);\n    }\n\n    // The C `main` returns 0; the process exit status is therefore always 0.\n    let _ = imp::run_main();\n}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
