{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_c64pyh7zqjgu75jzxts5poc65tqaxhzgccdro74mg4adorrss3ya","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. Both doc↔test diffs are empty and `verify.sh` reports **ALL CHECKS PASSED**.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe project turned out to be a single 51-line C translation unit (`driver` + `main`, reading `scanf(\"%d\")` and printing a 16-byte struct as hex), with **no** `[features]` table and no `#ifdef`s — so the feature matrix is exactly one combination (`{}`), asserted mechanically so it can't go stale.\n\n**Restructuring** — the crate only had a `[[bin]]`, so nothing could be loaded via `libloading`. I split the logic into `src/imp.rs`, added a `cdylib` (`src/lib.rs`) exporting `driver` and `main` with `#[no_mangle]`, and kept the binary mirroring `add_executable`. `libloading` went into `[dev-dependencies]`; `c_src/` was never touched (all builds go to `target/`).\n\n**Phase A artifacts**: `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md` (24 error rows + 6 FFI-boundary rows), `CONFIGS.md` (33 configuration rows), plus `verify.sh` which enumerates feature combos, builds both C artifacts, diffs symbols, and runs everything.\n\n**Phases B–D**: 64 differential tests, all loading **both** `.so`s through `dlopen`/`dlsym`. Symbol diff is empty (`driver`, `main`; `print_hex` stays `static` in both), and `RTLD_NOW` proves no unresolved symbols. Green in debug and release.\n\n**Seven real divergences found and fixed** (C is ground truth; every fix mutation-checked by reverting it and confirming the tests fail):\n\n| # | C behaviour | Rust before |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | dies from `SIGPIPE` (C startup disposition) | exited 0 — Rust installs `SIG_IGN` |\n| 2 | `scanf` `ungetc`s the terminating/mismatching char (`\"12x34\"` → 12 then fails forever) | consumed it, so a 2nd call read `34` |\n| 3 | EOF indicator is **sticky** | re-read and picked up appended data (`5,7,7,7` vs `5,0,0,0`) |\n| 4 | `stdin` shared with a host using C stdio (`\"5 7\"` → host 5, lib 7) | private `Stdin` saw a drained fd |\n| 5 | `exit` `lseek`s stdin back, so `{ driver; cat; } < f` leaves `\" hello world\\n\"` | `BufReader` swallowed 8 KiB |\n| 6 | `stdout` shared/fully buffered: ordered against host `printf`s, lost on `_exit` | wrote immediately, survived `_exit` |\n| 7 | 17 separate `printf`s → per-byte write/lock granularity | one atomic 33-byte write |\n\nDivergences 2–7 shared one root cause — the translation reimplemented `scanf`/`printf` on top of `std::io`. The fix was to **call the same libc functions the C calls**, so pushback, sticky EOF, `strtol` saturation, buffer sharing, the exit-time seek, ordering, durability and granularity are inherited exactly instead of approximated; the struct became `#[repr(C)]` walked via `size_of`, removing the hard-coded layout/endianness assumptions. Divergences 2–7 are reachable precisely because `main` and `driver` are exported symbols a consumer can call repeatedly or from a stdio-sharing host."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":804,"cache_read_input_tokens":253462,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":804,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f840c14a-a681-4b88-88b0-66464adaa41c","uuid":"8a4f21e2-fa1e-4d4d-ab69-05f2188e81fc","timestamp":"2026-08-18T10:56:59.636Z"}
